    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hyperalgesia and allodynia, two frequent symptoms of persistent pain, arise from either heightening the sensitivity of previous painful stimuli or making innocuous stimuli noxious, respectively. Understanding the neural circuitry mechanism underlying this persistent pain is of significant scientific and clinical interest. At the periphery, application of pro-inflammatory compounds lead to exaggerated responses to stimuli; while in the central nervous system, pro-inflammatory compounds suppress the inhibitory gate and exaggerate the responses to stimuli as well. Previous literature has shown that there are specific neural substrates that contribute to the gating of pain, but these studies rely on genetic strategies to ablate a large population of neurons, thereby creating a lack of specificity. We combined a novel in vivo two-photon spinal cord imaging preparation with the formalin injection model of persistent pain to systematically examine spinal neurons’ responses to thermal stimuli in a large population of neurons with single cell resolution. This platform allowed us to identify the specific populations of neurons that are modulated by pain and characterize their behaviors. We show that formalin produces warmth allodynia while decreasing the response to cold stimuli preferentially in broadly tuned cells. This increase in response to warmth and decrease in response to cold is partially mediated by the circuitry crosstalk of TRPV1 DRG inputs. Finally, we show that the application of glycine receptor antagonist, strychnine, and GABAA receptor antagonist, bicuculline, lead to an increase in neuronal response to temperature stimulation suggesting a critical role of the inhibitory circuitry in gating of pain.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
